4. Offering the Software as part of a Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) product.
5. Offering hosted, managed, cloud, or subscription-based access to the Software for payment.
6. Using the Software to provide paid consulting, implementation, support, hosting, development, or professional services where the Software is a material part of the service.
7. Incorporating the Software into a commercial product or proprietary product offered for sale or subscription.
8. Redistributing the Software as part of a commercial software bundle.
9. Selling modified versions, forks, ports, or derivative works.
10. Monetizing the Software through advertising, sponsorship, paid access, licensing fees, transaction fees, or other revenue-generating mechanisms.
11. Using the Software primarily to support a revenue-generating product or service.
12. Using the Software internally where the Software itself forms a material part of a commercial service delivered to customers.

For clarity, incidental use of general-purpose development tools by an individual does not automatically constitute Commercial Use. However, using this Software itself as part of a commercial product, commercial service, or revenue-generating offering is prohibited without a separate commercial license.

